<p class="copy">
	Built as tightly folded cities in space, a colony ship contains the prefabricated homes, construction facilities, vehicles, power systems, <?php echo sf_lore_link('lifters', 'lifters'); ?>, and defenses needed to kick-start a surface colony. Transported aboard the largest <?php echo sf_lore_link('stellar-carriers', 'Stellar Carriers'); ?>, a colony ship can quickly establish a viable, self-supporting habitat of sprawling interconnected habitation modules and industrial systems, connected by tunnel networks for safe transportation. Initial construction is highly automated, and pioneer settlers take several weeks to shuttle to the surface, growing the colony from a few hundred construction engineers into thousands of inhabitants. Once a viable colony has been established, the skeletal remains of the colony ship are deorbited and scrapped.
</p>
<p class="copy">
	Locations for colonial settlement are scouted, surveyed, and planned meticulously over years as investments and volunteers are secured. A successful colony can quickly grow to become the capital of an entire system, and many have long outlived the industries that originally brought them there. Colonies rarely fail due to the dangers of frontier life; rather, they fail to find a sustainable industrial niche and bleed colonists as their contracts expire. Indeed, there are many former boomtowns on rocks in faraway systems, left partially or even fully abandoned as their fortunes dried up and their investors cut their losses. Dying colonies can become hotbeds of crime and piracy as the remaining colonists' financial desperation pushes them to the brink.
</p>
